1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,536
If someone stole your container registry password tonight,

2
00:00:03,536 --> 00:00:07,095
could they run their own code in your production Kubernetes cluster?

3
00:00:07,633 --> 00:00:10,299
For most teams, the honest answer is yes.

4
00:00:10,299 --> 00:00:16,150
The cluster runs whatever image sits in the company registry, because the token was valid.

5
00:00:16,700 --> 00:00:20,577
In this video I will show you a project that changes that answer to no.

6
00:00:20,577 --> 00:00:24,946
Production only runs code that it can prove came from your own release pipeline.

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:27,139
And I do not just claim it.

8
00:00:27,139 --> 00:00:33,150
I attack it seven different ways on every single release, and I will show you the measured results.

9
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,944
By the end, you will understand every tool in this setup:

10
00:00:36,944 --> 00:00:41,951
what it does, how it is configured, and how to roll it out in a real production cluster.

11
00:00:41,951 --> 00:00:47,528
Everything is open source, and the full code and a free study guide are linked in the description.

12
00:00:49,267 --> 00:00:51,585
Here is what we will cover.

13
00:00:51,585 --> 00:00:57,596
First, why this problem matters, with real attacks from 2025 and 2026.

14
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:01,755
Then the idea behind the solution, and the architecture.

15
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:05,346
Then every tool, one by one.

16
00:01:05,346 --> 00:01:08,500
Docker and distroless images.

17
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:10,132
GitHub Actions.

18
00:01:10,132 --> 00:01:10,785
Trivy.

19
00:01:10,785 --> 00:01:11,329
Syft.

20
00:01:11,329 --> 00:01:13,069
SLSA provenance.

21
00:01:13,069 --> 00:01:15,244
And Sigstore cosign.

22
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:17,969
Kyverno for admission control.

23
00:01:17,969 --> 00:01:20,355
Argo CD and Kustomize for GitOps.

24
00:01:20,355 --> 00:01:24,260
Trivy Operator, Prometheus and Grafana for monitoring.

25
00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:29,105
And kind, for an end to end attack test that runs on every release.

26
00:01:29,667 --> 00:01:35,845
Finally, the measured results, a hands-on lab where you run and attack the whole platform on your own laptop,

27
00:01:35,845 --> 00:01:39,643
and a step by step plan to use this in your own production cluster.

28
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:43,195
Let us start with the problem.

29
00:01:43,195 --> 00:01:47,861
A Kubernetes cluster normally trusts any image it can pull from your registry.

30
00:01:47,861 --> 00:01:50,075
But a registry only proves one thing:

31
00:01:50,075 --> 00:01:52,947
that someone with a valid token pushed an image.

32
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:58,502
It does not prove that your reviewed code went into that image, or that your pipeline built it.

33
00:01:58,502 --> 00:02:01,819
And a vulnerability scan in your pipeline does not help either,

34
00:02:01,819 --> 00:02:04,819
if the image that runs is not the image that was scanned.

35
00:02:05,367 --> 00:02:06,734
Attackers know this.

36
00:02:06,734 --> 00:02:10,357
In 2025, the version tags of a popular GitHub Action,

37
00:02:10,357 --> 00:02:14,391
tj-actions changed files, were moved to a malicious commit.

38
00:02:14,391 --> 00:02:19,313
Thousands of pipelines ran the attacker's code and leaked their secrets.

39
00:02:19,867 --> 00:02:27,978
In 2026, the Megalodon campaign pushed malicious workflow files into more than five thousand repositories in about six hours.

40
00:02:27,978 --> 00:02:31,611
Those workflows ran with each repository's own identity.

41
00:02:32,167 --> 00:02:35,671
And the simplest attack of all is a leaked registry token.

42
00:02:35,671 --> 00:02:39,478
With it, anyone can push an image under the same name as yours.

43
00:02:39,478 --> 00:02:40,929
So the real question is:

44
00:02:40,929 --> 00:02:44,373
how can the cluster know where an image really came from?

45
00:02:46,133 --> 00:02:47,237
Here is the idea.

46
00:02:47,237 --> 00:02:48,535
Think of a pharmacy.

47
00:02:48,535 --> 00:02:53,339
A pharmacist does not hand out a medicine just because it is on the shelf.

48
00:02:53,339 --> 00:02:56,715
They check the factory seal and the paperwork first.

49
00:02:57,267 --> 00:02:59,643
We do the same with container images.

50
00:02:59,643 --> 00:03:02,469
Every image our pipeline builds gets a seal:

51
00:03:02,469 --> 00:03:06,066
a cryptographic signature, made by our release workflow.

52
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,581
Plus three signed documents.

53
00:03:08,581 --> 00:03:11,977
First, an origin certificate, called provenance,

54
00:03:11,977 --> 00:03:16,505
that says which repository, branch and commit it was built from.

55
00:03:17,067 --> 00:03:18,742
Second, a lab test:

56
00:03:18,742 --> 00:03:21,211
a signed vulnerability scan.

57
00:03:21,767 --> 00:03:23,666
Third, an ingredient list:

58
00:03:23,666 --> 00:03:29,582
a software bill of materials, or SBOM, that lists every library inside the image.

59
00:03:30,133 --> 00:03:33,424
At the door of the cluster sits the pharmacist:

60
00:03:33,424 --> 00:03:33,985
Kyverno.

61
00:03:33,985 --> 00:03:38,606
Before any pod starts, it checks the seal and all three documents.

62
00:03:38,606 --> 00:03:40,286
No valid papers, no pod.

63
00:03:40,286 --> 00:03:44,768
It does not matter who pushed the image, or how it got into git.

64
00:03:46,533 --> 00:03:48,353
Here is the whole architecture.

65
00:03:48,353 --> 00:03:52,345
It starts when a reviewed pull request is merged to the main branch.

66
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:56,386
The release workflow in GitHub Actions builds the image,

67
00:03:56,386 --> 00:04:01,926
runs the unit tests, scans it, and pushes it to the GitHub container registry, by digest.

68
00:04:02,467 --> 00:04:08,273
Then it creates the SBOM, the scan report and the provenance, and signs everything with Sigstore.

69
00:04:08,273 --> 00:04:10,248
There is no private key anywhere.

70
00:04:10,248 --> 00:04:16,473
GitHub vouches for the workflow's identity, and every signature is written to a public log called Rekor.

71
00:04:17,033 --> 00:04:22,182
Next, an end to end test creates a brand new Kubernetes cluster and attacks the new release.

72
00:04:22,182 --> 00:04:28,058
Only if every attack fails does the pipeline commit the new image digest to the production folder in git.

73
00:04:28,600 --> 00:04:32,035
Argo CD sees that commit and applies it to the cluster.

74
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:38,216
Every pod passes through Kyverno, which verifies the signature and the three documents.

75
00:04:38,216 --> 00:04:39,960
Only then does the pod run.

76
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:43,437
And because new vulnerabilities are published every day,

77
00:04:43,437 --> 00:04:46,008
Trivy Operator keeps scanning the running images,

78
00:04:46,008 --> 00:04:50,151
while Prometheus and Grafana alert on blocked attempts and new critical issues.

79
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:55,742
Let us go through every tool, starting with what we ship:

80
00:04:55,742 --> 00:04:58,506
a small Go service called storefront API.

81
00:04:58,506 --> 00:05:00,933
The Dockerfile is a two stage build.

82
00:05:00,933 --> 00:05:05,450
Notice that both base images are pinned by digest, not only by tag.

83
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,511
The unit tests run inside the build, so a failing test means no image.

84
00:05:10,511 --> 00:05:16,504
The binary is static, built with trim path and an empty build ID, so builds are reproducible.

85
00:05:17,067 --> 00:05:19,544
The final image is distroless:

86
00:05:19,544 --> 00:05:25,406
no shell, no package manager, and it runs as user 65532, never as root.

87
00:05:25,967 --> 00:05:28,374
The whole image is only 3.7 megabytes.

88
00:05:28,374 --> 00:05:31,352
Why does this matter for supply chain security?

89
00:05:31,352 --> 00:05:34,139
Fewer components mean fewer vulnerabilities,

90
00:05:34,139 --> 00:05:39,461
which keeps the scan gate green, and leaves an attacker almost nothing to work with.

91
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:42,639
Next, GitHub Actions.

92
00:05:42,639 --> 00:05:44,625
This is the release workflow.

93
00:05:44,625 --> 00:05:48,530
And here is the most important idea of the whole project:

94
00:05:48,530 --> 00:05:51,202
this workflow file is the trust anchor.

95
00:05:51,202 --> 00:05:53,668
Production trusts exactly one thing:

96
00:05:53,668 --> 00:05:57,025
signatures made by this file, on the main branch.

97
00:05:57,567 --> 00:05:59,952
At the top, permissions is empty.

98
00:05:59,952 --> 00:06:04,001
No job gets any access unless it explicitly asks for it.

99
00:06:04,567 --> 00:06:07,408
The build job asks for exactly three things:

100
00:06:07,408 --> 00:06:12,058
read the code, write packages so it can push the image, and an ID token.

101
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:15,904
That ID token is what makes keyless signing possible.

102
00:06:15,904 --> 00:06:18,647
GitHub issues a short lived token that says:

103
00:06:18,647 --> 00:06:23,260
I am the release workflow, of this repository, running on the main branch.

104
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:25,661
Then the jobs run in order:

105
00:06:25,661 --> 00:06:29,176
build and sign, then the attack test, then promote.

106
00:06:29,176 --> 00:06:32,139
Promote needs both earlier jobs to succeed.

107
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:38,721
Because the pipeline is the trust anchor, the pipeline itself is hardened.

108
00:06:38,721 --> 00:06:42,630
This is exactly where the 2025 tag attack would have failed.

109
00:06:43,167 --> 00:06:47,023
Every action is pinned to a full commit SHA, not to a tag.

110
00:06:47,023 --> 00:06:49,682
A tag can be moved, a commit SHA cannot.

111
00:06:49,682 --> 00:06:54,403
A small script in CI fails the build if anyone adds an unpinned action.

112
00:06:54,967 --> 00:06:58,046
No expressions are placed directly inside run blocks.

113
00:06:58,046 --> 00:07:00,835
Values are passed through environment variables,

114
00:07:00,835 --> 00:07:05,018
which closes the template injection hole that many workflow attacks use.

115
00:07:05,567 --> 00:07:08,201
Checkout does not leave its token on disk.

116
00:07:08,201 --> 00:07:12,904
And tools like cosign, Syft and Trivy are downloaded with a checksum check,

117
00:07:12,904 --> 00:07:15,601
instead of adding more third party actions.

118
00:07:16,167 --> 00:07:20,837
On every push, zizmor and actionlint scan the workflows for security mistakes.

119
00:07:20,837 --> 00:07:26,046
And CODEOWNERS plus Dependabot make sure changes are reviewed and pins stay up to date.

120
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:31,328
Tool number three is Trivy, a vulnerability scanner.

121
00:07:31,328 --> 00:07:33,363
It runs twice in the pipeline.

122
00:07:33,363 --> 00:07:34,449
First as a gate.

123
00:07:34,449 --> 00:07:38,520
If the image has any fixable critical or high vulnerability,

124
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:42,251
exit code one stops the release, and nothing is pushed.

125
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:47,439
Second, after the push, Trivy writes its report in the cosign vuln format.

126
00:07:47,439 --> 00:07:53,206
That report gets signed, so the cluster can read a scan result that nobody could have faked.

127
00:07:53,767 --> 00:07:54,353
Why both?

128
00:07:54,353 --> 00:07:56,959
The gate gives developers fast feedback.

129
00:07:56,959 --> 00:08:02,561
The signed report is evidence that travels with the image, all the way to the cluster.

130
00:08:04,300 --> 00:08:08,557
Tool four is Syft, which creates the software bill of materials.

131
00:08:08,557 --> 00:08:14,941
It reads the pushed image, by digest, and lists every component inside, in the CycloneDX format.

132
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:16,698
Why do we need it?

133
00:08:16,698 --> 00:08:21,291
When the next big vulnerability is announced, the question is always:

134
00:08:21,291 --> 00:08:22,356
are we affected?

135
00:08:22,356 --> 00:08:27,815
With an SBOM for every image, the answer is a search, not a week of investigation.

136
00:08:28,367 --> 00:08:32,433
Regulations like the EU Cyber Resilience Act also ask for SBOMs.

137
00:08:32,433 --> 00:08:38,341
Here the SBOM is signed and attached to the image, and Kyverno refuses any image without one.

138
00:08:40,100 --> 00:08:44,342
Tool five is provenance, in the SLSA version one format.

139
00:08:44,342 --> 00:08:50,175
SLSA, pronounced salsa, is a framework that describes how software was built.

140
00:08:50,733 --> 00:08:56,327
Our provenance records the repository, the branch, and the workflow file that ran the build.

141
00:08:56,327 --> 00:09:01,130
Later, Kyverno checks that the repository is ours, and that the branch is main.

142
00:09:01,667 --> 00:09:05,660
It also records the exact git commit, and a link to the workflow run,

143
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:09,826
so anyone can trace a running pod back to the line of code it came from.

144
00:09:10,367 --> 00:09:12,775
On its own, this is just a JSON file.

145
00:09:12,775 --> 00:09:15,574
What makes it trustworthy is the next tool:

146
00:09:15,574 --> 00:09:20,586
it is signed by the release workflow, so nobody else can produce a valid one.

147
00:09:22,333 --> 00:09:24,383
Now the heart of the project:

148
00:09:24,383 --> 00:09:27,494
Sigstore, and its command line tool, cosign.

149
00:09:27,494 --> 00:09:30,039
Classic signing needs a private key.

150
00:09:30,039 --> 00:09:33,574
Someone has to store it, protect it and rotate it.

151
00:09:33,574 --> 00:09:36,048
And a stolen key can sign anything.

152
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:38,663
Keyless signing removes the key.

153
00:09:38,663 --> 00:09:44,014
The workflow shows its GitHub ID token to Fulcio, Sigstore's certificate authority.

154
00:09:44,567 --> 00:09:48,717
Fulcio issues a certificate that is valid for only ten minutes.

155
00:09:48,717 --> 00:09:51,880
Inside the certificate is the workflow identity:

156
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:55,438
this repository, release dot yaml, on the main branch.

157
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:59,246
cosign signs the image digest with that certificate,

158
00:09:59,246 --> 00:10:04,926
stores the signature next to the image, and records it in Rekor, a public transparency log.

159
00:10:04,926 --> 00:10:09,046
Anyone can audit that log, and nobody can quietly remove an entry.

160
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:12,151
In the workflow, that is four commands:

161
00:10:12,151 --> 00:10:17,579
sign the image, then attest the provenance, the vulnerability report, and the SBOM.

162
00:10:17,579 --> 00:10:20,129
One detail cost me real debugging time:

163
00:10:20,129 --> 00:10:23,988
cosign version three writes a new bundle format by default,

164
00:10:23,988 --> 00:10:27,846
and Kyverno 1.19 did not find those signatures in my tests.

165
00:10:27,846 --> 00:10:32,882
So the pipeline uses the classic format, with new bundle format set to false.

166
00:10:34,633 --> 00:10:36,513
Tool seven is the registry:

167
00:10:36,513 --> 00:10:38,603
the GitHub container registry.

168
00:10:38,603 --> 00:10:42,084
The important concept here is tags versus digests.

169
00:10:42,084 --> 00:10:44,939
A tag, like version one, is just a label.

170
00:10:44,939 --> 00:10:49,048
Anyone with write access can point it at different content.

171
00:10:49,600 --> 00:10:53,656
A digest is the SHA 256 hash of the image content.

172
00:10:53,656 --> 00:10:56,900
If one byte changes, the digest changes.

173
00:10:56,900 --> 00:11:03,065
So signatures are made on digests, git stores digests, and pods run digests.

174
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:08,584
The signature and the three attestations are stored right next to the image in the same registry,

175
00:11:08,584 --> 00:11:10,896
so the proof travels wherever the image goes.

176
00:11:12,633 --> 00:11:14,959
Tool eight is the guard at the door:

177
00:11:14,959 --> 00:11:15,476
Kyverno.

178
00:11:15,476 --> 00:11:20,258
Every request to create or change a pod goes to the Kubernetes API server.

179
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:26,454
Before the object is stored, the API server asks admission webhooks for their opinion.

180
00:11:26,454 --> 00:11:27,966
Kyverno is one of them.

181
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:31,668
If Kyverno says no, the pod is never created.

182
00:11:31,668 --> 00:11:38,919
It does not matter whether the request came from a person with kubectl, from a CI job, or from Argo CD.

183
00:11:39,467 --> 00:11:43,087
This project uses the new Kyverno 1.19 policy types,

184
00:11:43,087 --> 00:11:47,822
written in CEL, the same expression language Kubernetes itself uses.

185
00:11:47,822 --> 00:11:50,816
The older ClusterPolicy type is deprecated.

186
00:11:52,567 --> 00:11:54,323
Here is the main policy:

187
00:11:54,323 --> 00:11:55,933
verify release images.

188
00:11:55,933 --> 00:12:00,690
It only applies to namespaces with the label enforce equals true.

189
00:12:00,690 --> 00:12:03,471
So you decide, namespace by namespace.

190
00:12:04,033 --> 00:12:06,434
The attestor is the most important part.

191
00:12:06,434 --> 00:12:06,914
It says:

192
00:12:06,914 --> 00:12:11,056
the signature must come from a Sigstore certificate issued by GitHub,

193
00:12:11,056 --> 00:12:15,317
for the subject release dot yaml on the main branch of this repository.

194
00:12:15,317 --> 00:12:17,778
And it must be recorded in the Rekor log.

195
00:12:18,333 --> 00:12:21,347
Then we declare the three documents we expect:

196
00:12:21,347 --> 00:12:24,950
the provenance, the vulnerability report, and the SBOM.

197
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:29,755
Mutate digest rewrites the pod's image to the exact digest that was verified.

198
00:12:29,755 --> 00:12:33,845
So nobody can move a tag between the check and the start of the container.

199
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:36,131
Finally, the validations.

200
00:12:36,131 --> 00:12:37,932
The signature must verify.

201
00:12:37,932 --> 00:12:41,395
The provenance must name this repository and main.

202
00:12:41,395 --> 00:12:45,204
The signed scan must have no critical or high findings.

203
00:12:45,204 --> 00:12:47,351
And the signed SBOM must exist.

204
00:12:47,351 --> 00:12:52,406
Every rule has a clear message, so a refused developer knows exactly why.

205
00:12:54,167 --> 00:12:56,578
Two more policies close the simple gaps.

206
00:12:56,578 --> 00:13:01,884
Allowed images says that pods in production may only use images from our own repository.

207
00:13:01,884 --> 00:13:07,371
A Docker Hub image is refused in about half a second, before any signature is even checked.

208
00:13:07,933 --> 00:13:10,685
It also requires workloads stored in git,

209
00:13:10,685 --> 00:13:15,518
like Deployments, to reference images by digest, never by a movable tag.

210
00:13:16,067 --> 00:13:21,263
Restricted pods covers the case where a trusted image is started in a dangerous way:

211
00:13:21,263 --> 00:13:25,346
as root, privileged, with host access, or without resource limits.

212
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:29,761
The production namespace switches all of this on with two labels.

213
00:13:29,761 --> 00:13:35,641
One for our Kyverno policies, and one for the Pod Security admission that is built into Kubernetes,

214
00:13:35,641 --> 00:13:37,067
at the restricted level.

215
00:13:37,067 --> 00:13:39,383
Two independent locks on the same door.

216
00:13:41,133 --> 00:13:45,873
How do you introduce rules like these without breaking every team on day one?

217
00:13:45,873 --> 00:13:46,858
With audit mode.

218
00:13:47,400 --> 00:13:51,640
A Kustomize overlay creates a copy of every policy with the suffix audit,

219
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:56,229
and patches the action from Deny to Audit, for namespaces with the label audit.

220
00:13:56,767 --> 00:13:58,794
In audit mode nothing is blocked.

221
00:13:58,794 --> 00:14:02,174
Violations show up in policy reports and in Prometheus.

222
00:14:02,174 --> 00:14:08,011
Teams fix what the reports find, and then you switch the namespace label from audit to enforce.

223
00:14:08,567 --> 00:14:12,492
In the test, an unsigned image runs fine in the sandbox namespace,

224
00:14:12,492 --> 00:14:16,417
and the policy reports list its violations eighteen seconds later.

225
00:14:18,167 --> 00:14:21,203
Tool nine is GitOps, with Argo CD and Kustomize.

226
00:14:21,203 --> 00:14:26,200
The production folder in git is the single source of truth for what should run.

227
00:14:26,733 --> 00:14:30,068
The production kustomization pins the image by digest.

228
00:14:30,068 --> 00:14:35,440
Only the release pipeline changes this line, and only after the attack test has passed.

229
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:41,639
Argo CD watches that folder on main, with automated sync, prune and self heal.

230
00:14:41,639 --> 00:14:46,049
If someone changes the cluster by hand, Argo CD puts it back.

231
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:48,898
And here is a key design decision:

232
00:14:48,898 --> 00:14:51,398
Argo CD is not trusted with security.

233
00:14:51,398 --> 00:14:58,426
If an attacker manages to commit a bad digest, Argo CD will try to apply it, and Kyverno will refuse it.

234
00:14:58,426 --> 00:15:00,995
We test exactly that on every release.

235
00:15:02,733 --> 00:15:04,098
This is the promote job.

236
00:15:04,098 --> 00:15:07,682
It only runs when the build and the attack test both succeeded,

237
00:15:07,682 --> 00:15:10,753
and its only permission is to write to the repository.

238
00:15:11,300 --> 00:15:15,323
It replaces the digest in the production kustomization with the new,

239
00:15:15,323 --> 00:15:18,695
verified digest, and commits it to main as a release bot.

240
00:15:19,233 --> 00:15:21,263
That commit is the deployment.

241
00:15:21,263 --> 00:15:23,631
To roll back, you simply revert it.

242
00:15:23,631 --> 00:15:29,383
Argo CD then deploys the previous digest, which is still signed and still verifiable.

243
00:15:31,133 --> 00:15:33,706
Signing proves where an image came from.

244
00:15:33,706 --> 00:15:38,467
It does not protect you from a vulnerability that is published next month.

245
00:15:38,467 --> 00:15:40,268
That is the job of tool ten:

246
00:15:40,268 --> 00:15:41,233
Trivy Operator.

247
00:15:41,767 --> 00:15:48,603
It runs inside the cluster, watches the running workloads in prod and in sandbox, and re-scans their images.

248
00:15:48,603 --> 00:15:52,718
The results become vulnerability reports, and Prometheus metrics.

249
00:15:53,267 --> 00:15:57,136
In the test, an old nginx image is started in the sandbox.

250
00:15:57,136 --> 00:16:02,940
Trivy Operator found twenty seven critical vulnerabilities in it, ninety seconds later.

251
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:06,324
Tool eleven is Prometheus.

252
00:16:06,324 --> 00:16:12,259
It collects the metrics from Kyverno and from Trivy Operator, and three alert rules watch them.

253
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:17,200
Untrusted workload blocked fires when anything is refused in production.

254
00:16:17,200 --> 00:16:21,966
In a healthy pipeline that never happens, so even one refusal is worth a look.

255
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:29,144
Running image has critical vulnerabilities fires when Trivy Operator finds critical issues in a running image for five minutes.

256
00:16:29,700 --> 00:16:34,453
And admission controller down fires when Kyverno itself disappears.

257
00:16:34,453 --> 00:16:36,653
Remember, Kyverno fails closed:

258
00:16:36,653 --> 00:16:39,419
when it is down, no new pods can start.

259
00:16:39,967 --> 00:16:44,089
All three rules have unit tests that run with promtool in CI.

260
00:16:44,089 --> 00:16:48,143
And those tests, together with a live demo, found real bugs.

261
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:52,492
Here is an honest lesson from building this.

262
00:16:52,492 --> 00:16:57,559
My first version of the alert only counted refusals from Kyverno's validating webhook.

263
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:01,658
But the image policy also rewrites images to their digest,

264
00:17:01,658 --> 00:17:05,094
so signature failures are refused in the mutating phase.

265
00:17:05,094 --> 00:17:08,345
The alert missed exactly the most important refusals:

266
00:17:08,345 --> 00:17:10,124
unsigned and imposter images.

267
00:17:10,124 --> 00:17:13,866
A local demo showed ten refusals where nineteen had happened.

268
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:15,290
The second bug:

269
00:17:15,290 --> 00:17:19,147
Prometheus' increase function cannot see the very first refusals,

270
00:17:19,147 --> 00:17:24,071
because Kyverno creates the counter when it already has a value, for example three.

271
00:17:24,633 --> 00:17:28,100
Both are fixed, and both cases now have unit tests.

272
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:29,528
The lesson is simple:

273
00:17:29,528 --> 00:17:34,558
test your alerts against real attacks, not just against your expectations.

274
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:38,769
Grafana shows it all on one dashboard.

275
00:17:38,769 --> 00:17:41,043
This is the local lab after a demo.

276
00:17:41,043 --> 00:17:41,758
At the top:

277
00:17:41,758 --> 00:17:45,201
what was blocked in production, the signature checks,

278
00:17:45,201 --> 00:17:49,620
running images with critical vulnerabilities, and the firing alerts.

279
00:17:50,167 --> 00:17:53,493
Below, refusals over time, image verification results,

280
00:17:53,493 --> 00:17:57,805
vulnerabilities per image, and the health of the admission controller.

281
00:17:57,805 --> 00:18:03,411
The dashboard is provisioned from a JSON file in git, like everything else in this project.

282
00:18:05,167 --> 00:18:07,706
Tool twelve is kind, Kubernetes in Docker.

283
00:18:07,706 --> 00:18:10,910
It powers the part that makes this project different:

284
00:18:10,910 --> 00:18:16,896
an end to end attack test that runs on every release, on a brand new cluster inside GitHub Actions.

285
00:18:17,433 --> 00:18:20,162
Section one builds the whole platform:

286
00:18:20,162 --> 00:18:25,260
Kyverno, the policies, Argo CD, Trivy Operator, Prometheus and Grafana.

287
00:18:25,260 --> 00:18:28,133
That takes about two and a half minutes.

288
00:18:28,667 --> 00:18:33,246
Section two deploys the new signed release the real way, through Argo CD.

289
00:18:33,800 --> 00:18:36,812
Section three attacks the cluster door directly.

290
00:18:36,812 --> 00:18:41,895
It pushes attacker images into our real registry, which simulates a stolen token.

291
00:18:42,433 --> 00:18:47,198
Section four attacks through git, with a malicious commit to the production folder.

292
00:18:47,198 --> 00:18:52,020
Section five checks audit mode and live scanning, and section six checks the alerts.

293
00:18:52,567 --> 00:18:57,207
If anything is not refused, the job fails, and the promote job never runs.

294
00:18:57,207 --> 00:19:01,158
A release that weakens the defences can never reach production.

295
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:05,878
The most interesting attack is the imposter.

296
00:19:05,878 --> 00:19:07,841
It copies the Megalodon idea:

297
00:19:07,841 --> 00:19:12,376
a second workflow file in the same repository, called e2e dot yaml.

298
00:19:12,933 --> 00:19:18,214
That workflow takes a tampered image and signs it with a completely valid Sigstore signature.

299
00:19:18,214 --> 00:19:21,621
It even attaches forged provenance and a clean looking scan.

300
00:19:21,621 --> 00:19:25,028
The test first verifies that this signature really is valid.

301
00:19:25,567 --> 00:19:29,259
Kyverno still refuses it, in about two seconds.

302
00:19:29,259 --> 00:19:29,574
Why?

303
00:19:29,574 --> 00:19:33,973
The certificate says e2e dot yaml, not release dot yaml.

304
00:19:33,973 --> 00:19:36,645
Signed is not the same as trusted.

305
00:19:36,645 --> 00:19:38,923
The policy checks who signed.

306
00:19:40,667 --> 00:19:46,230
So here are the measured results, from a real GitHub Actions run that is linked in the description.

307
00:19:46,230 --> 00:19:49,096
The times show how long the cluster took to say no.

308
00:19:49,633 --> 00:19:52,323
An image from another registry:

309
00:19:52,323 --> 00:19:54,753
refused in 484 milliseconds.

310
00:19:55,300 --> 00:19:58,566
An unsigned image pushed with a stolen token:

311
00:19:58,566 --> 00:20:00,670
refused in about two seconds.

312
00:20:00,670 --> 00:20:02,775
A tampered copy of our image:

313
00:20:02,775 --> 00:20:03,645
two seconds.

314
00:20:03,645 --> 00:20:06,621
And the imposter, with a valid signature:

315
00:20:06,621 --> 00:20:07,855
also two seconds.

316
00:20:08,400 --> 00:20:12,180
Our own image started as a privileged container:

317
00:20:12,180 --> 00:20:15,725
refused in 363 milliseconds, by Pod Security.

318
00:20:16,267 --> 00:20:17,847
The malicious git commit:

319
00:20:17,847 --> 00:20:22,524
Argo CD applied it, and the cluster refused it six seconds after the push.

320
00:20:22,524 --> 00:20:24,990
Production kept serving the whole time.

321
00:20:25,533 --> 00:20:30,684
And the old nginx image with known vulnerabilities was found, and its alert fired.

322
00:20:30,684 --> 00:20:34,390
Meanwhile, the real release was admitted every single time.

323
00:20:36,133 --> 00:20:38,028
Now let us make this practical.

324
00:20:38,028 --> 00:20:43,161
You can run the whole platform on your own laptop, for free, and attack it yourself.

325
00:20:43,161 --> 00:20:48,416
I recorded every command and output in this part from a fresh clone of the repository,

326
00:20:48,416 --> 00:20:50,800
so you will see exactly what to expect.

327
00:20:51,333 --> 00:20:56,393
You need Docker, with at least four CPUs and eight gigabytes of memory available.

328
00:20:56,933 --> 00:21:02,367
You need kind, which creates a Kubernetes cluster inside Docker, and git.

329
00:21:02,367 --> 00:21:04,154
Any Bash terminal works:

330
00:21:04,154 --> 00:21:06,908
macOS, Linux, or Git Bash on Windows.

331
00:21:07,467 --> 00:21:15,252
And an internet connection, because the cluster pulls the signed release from the registry and checks its signatures with Sigstore.

332
00:21:15,252 --> 00:21:18,877
There is no cloud account to create, and nothing costs money.

333
00:21:18,877 --> 00:21:25,058
Every other tool, like kubectl, Helm and cosign, runs inside a container that the scripts build for you.

334
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:27,457
Step one:

335
00:21:27,457 --> 00:21:32,786
clone the repository, go into the folder, and run the platform up script.

336
00:21:32,786 --> 00:21:35,049
That is the only setup command.

337
00:21:35,600 --> 00:21:38,759
First it builds the toolbox image, with kubectl,

338
00:21:38,759 --> 00:21:44,222
Helm, cosign, crane and the Kyverno command line, all pinned and checksum verified.

339
00:21:44,222 --> 00:21:48,829
Then it creates a kind cluster with a control plane and a worker node.

340
00:21:49,367 --> 00:21:52,095
Next it installs Kyverno and the policies,

341
00:21:52,095 --> 00:21:55,734
labels the namespaces, installs Argo CD, Trivy Operator,

342
00:21:55,734 --> 00:22:01,906
Prometheus and Grafana, and finally points Argo CD at the production folder on the main branch.

343
00:22:02,467 --> 00:22:07,729
From a fresh clone on my laptop, this took three minutes and thirty seven seconds.

344
00:22:07,729 --> 00:22:11,323
A minute later, Argo CD has deployed the signed release.

345
00:22:13,067 --> 00:22:13,781
Step two:

346
00:22:13,781 --> 00:22:14,734
look around.

347
00:22:14,734 --> 00:22:17,117
Every exercise is one command:

348
00:22:17,117 --> 00:22:22,199
scripts slash try dot S H, followed by the name of the exercise.

349
00:22:22,199 --> 00:22:23,628
Start with status.

350
00:22:24,167 --> 00:22:25,670
You see the platform:

351
00:22:25,670 --> 00:22:28,606
Argo CD, Kyverno, Grafana and Prometheus,

352
00:22:28,606 --> 00:22:33,546
Trivy Operator, and our storefront API running in the prod namespace.

353
00:22:34,100 --> 00:22:35,633
There are six policies:

354
00:22:35,633 --> 00:22:39,899
our three, plus an audit copy of each for the sandbox namespace.

355
00:22:40,433 --> 00:22:44,891
And the release in prod is synced and healthy, and pinned to a digest.

356
00:22:44,891 --> 00:22:49,221
Argo CD deployed it from git, and Kyverno verified it on the way in.

357
00:22:50,967 --> 00:22:51,840
Step three:

358
00:22:51,840 --> 00:22:56,761
verify the release yourself, exactly the way the cluster does.

359
00:22:56,761 --> 00:22:58,586
Run try dot S H verify.

360
00:22:59,133 --> 00:23:03,906
cosign checks the signature, the certificate from Sigstore's certificate authority,

361
00:23:03,906 --> 00:23:06,493
and the entry in the public transparency log.

362
00:23:07,033 --> 00:23:13,394
It tells you the exact commit and workflow that built the image, and that all three attestations are signed:

363
00:23:13,394 --> 00:23:16,280
provenance, the vulnerability scan, and the SBOM.

364
00:23:16,833 --> 00:23:18,456
Now run wrong identity.

365
00:23:18,456 --> 00:23:24,452
It does the same check, but expects a different workflow, ci dot yaml, as the signer.

366
00:23:24,452 --> 00:23:27,414
It fails, and shows you who really signed:

367
00:23:27,414 --> 00:23:29,178
release dot yaml on main.

368
00:23:29,178 --> 00:23:32,705
This one check is what stops an injected workflow.

369
00:23:34,467 --> 00:23:35,236
Step four:

370
00:23:35,236 --> 00:23:36,467
be the attacker.

371
00:23:36,467 --> 00:23:37,852
First the control:

372
00:23:37,852 --> 00:23:42,699
try dot S H trusted starts the signed release as a pod in prod.

373
00:23:42,699 --> 00:23:43,853
It is admitted.

374
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:45,263
Now foreign:

375
00:23:45,263 --> 00:23:47,494
an nginx image from Docker Hub.

376
00:23:47,494 --> 00:23:52,099
Refused by allowed images, with a message that says exactly why.

377
00:23:52,633 --> 00:23:54,141
Unsigned is a sneaky one.

378
00:23:54,141 --> 00:23:58,181
It builds your own image on your laptop, gives it our trusted name,

379
00:23:58,181 --> 00:24:02,582
and side loads it straight onto the cluster nodes, skipping the registry.

380
00:24:02,582 --> 00:24:03,728
Kyverno refuses it:

381
00:24:03,728 --> 00:24:07,768
it insists on finding the image and its signatures in the registry,

382
00:24:07,768 --> 00:24:10,602
and an image it cannot verify there never runs.

383
00:24:11,167 --> 00:24:15,752
And privileged starts our own, trusted image as a privileged container.

384
00:24:15,752 --> 00:24:19,886
Pod Security refuses it, before Kyverno even looks at the image.

385
00:24:21,633 --> 00:24:22,278
Step five:

386
00:24:22,278 --> 00:24:22,988
audit mode.

387
00:24:22,988 --> 00:24:28,017
try dot S H sandbox starts the same Docker Hub image in the sandbox namespace.

388
00:24:28,017 --> 00:24:34,208
This time it is admitted, and a moment later the policy report says it would be refused in prod.

389
00:24:34,208 --> 00:24:38,013
This is how you introduce policies without breaking anyone.

390
00:24:38,567 --> 00:24:43,925
Then scan starts an old nginx, version 1.21.0, and waits for Trivy Operator.

391
00:24:43,925 --> 00:24:50,481
On my laptop it found twenty seven critical and one hundred thirty four high vulnerabilities.

392
00:24:51,033 --> 00:24:53,738
And alerts shows what Prometheus thinks.

393
00:24:53,738 --> 00:24:58,808
Untrusted workload blocked is firing, because of your attacks in step four.

394
00:24:58,808 --> 00:25:01,445
And the vulnerability alert is pending:

395
00:25:01,445 --> 00:25:06,583
even today's nginx 1.29 from the sandbox exercise has six critical findings.

396
00:25:06,583 --> 00:25:08,476
It fires after five minutes.

397
00:25:10,233 --> 00:25:10,947
Step six.

398
00:25:10,947 --> 00:25:18,958
try dot S H dashboard opens Grafana on localhost, port 3000, with the same dashboard you saw earlier.

399
00:25:19,500 --> 00:25:22,638
To run your own commands, source lib dot S H.

400
00:25:22,638 --> 00:25:26,614
Then k is kubectl, and tb runs any tool from the toolbox,

401
00:25:26,614 --> 00:25:32,611
for example cosign tree, to see every signature and attestation attached to the image.

402
00:25:33,167 --> 00:25:36,628
When you are ready for more, the study guide has nine labs.

403
00:25:36,628 --> 00:25:39,327
You break a policy and let the tests catch it,

404
00:25:39,327 --> 00:25:41,791
break an alert and let prom tool catch it,

405
00:25:41,791 --> 00:25:46,308
fix a vulnerable workflow, and follow a full signed release in your own fork.

406
00:25:46,867 --> 00:25:52,230
When you are done, clean up the exercise pods, and delete the cluster with kind.

407
00:25:52,230 --> 00:25:54,241
Your laptop is back to normal.

408
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:59,630
Now, how do you use this in your own production?

409
00:25:59,630 --> 00:26:01,975
Here is the plan, step by step.

410
00:26:02,533 --> 00:26:03,129
Step one:

411
00:26:03,129 --> 00:26:04,054
make it yours.

412
00:26:04,054 --> 00:26:08,882
Change the image name and the trusted identity in the three policy files,

413
00:26:08,882 --> 00:26:12,056
in the release workflow, and in the test script.

414
00:26:12,600 --> 00:26:13,159
Step two:

415
00:26:13,159 --> 00:26:14,713
protect the trust anchor.

416
00:26:14,713 --> 00:26:19,561
Turn on branch protection for main, with required reviews and required checks,

417
00:26:19,561 --> 00:26:22,917
and use CODEOWNERS for the workflows and the policies.

418
00:26:22,917 --> 00:26:26,956
Whoever can change release dot yaml on main can release anything.

419
00:26:27,500 --> 00:26:28,251
Step three:

420
00:26:28,251 --> 00:26:30,025
install Kyverno with Helm.

421
00:26:30,025 --> 00:26:36,715
In production, run at least three admission controller replicas, because the webhook fails closed.

422
00:26:37,267 --> 00:26:37,950
Step four:

423
00:26:37,950 --> 00:26:39,317
start in audit mode.

424
00:26:39,317 --> 00:26:46,356
Label your namespaces with audit, read the policy reports for one or two weeks, and fix what they find.

425
00:26:48,100 --> 00:26:48,778
Step five:

426
00:26:48,778 --> 00:26:54,885
switch to enforce gradually, one namespace at a time, starting with the most critical one.

427
00:26:55,433 --> 00:26:55,967
Step six:

428
00:26:55,967 --> 00:27:01,363
route the three alerts to your on call channel, and put the dashboard in front of the team.

429
00:27:01,900 --> 00:27:02,708
Step seven:

430
00:27:02,708 --> 00:27:08,215
for real emergencies, use a reviewed, time limited policy exception in git.

431
00:27:08,215 --> 00:27:10,125
Never delete the policies.

432
00:27:10,667 --> 00:27:12,855
And step eight, day two operations:

433
00:27:12,855 --> 00:27:16,855
a rollback is a git revert, there are no signing keys to rotate,

434
00:27:16,855 --> 00:27:21,106
and to stop trusting a build, you change the identity in the policy.

435
00:27:22,867 --> 00:27:25,156
Let us be honest about the limits.

436
00:27:25,156 --> 00:27:30,945
The build job also signs, so this is SLSA level two style provenance, not level three.

437
00:27:30,945 --> 00:27:34,446
The next step is the official SLSA GitHub generator.

438
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:40,245
Admission control does not protect against cluster admins, who can delete the policies.

439
00:27:40,245 --> 00:27:45,550
And the public Sigstore service is used, so air gapped clusters need a private Sigstore.

440
00:27:46,100 --> 00:27:47,085
Also on the list:

441
00:27:47,085 --> 00:27:50,328
the new Sigstore bundle format once Kyverno supports it,

442
00:27:50,328 --> 00:27:54,093
and verifying the signatures of the base images during the build.

443
00:27:55,833 --> 00:27:57,058
That is the project.

444
00:27:57,058 --> 00:28:01,161
Production now runs only code it can prove came from your pipeline,

445
00:28:01,161 --> 00:28:04,468
and every release proves it again by attacking itself.

446
00:28:05,033 --> 00:28:09,077
The code, the documentation and a free sixty page study guide,

447
00:28:09,077 --> 00:28:13,317
with labs and interview questions, are linked in the description.

448
00:28:13,317 --> 00:28:16,970
Clone it, run platform up, and try the attacks yourself.

449
00:28:17,533 --> 00:28:19,736
Now I would like to hear from you:

450
00:28:19,736 --> 00:28:24,529
with your registry password, could someone run their code in your cluster?

451
00:28:24,529 --> 00:28:26,083
Tell me in the comments.

452
00:28:26,083 --> 00:28:29,969
And if this helped, subscribe for more real DevOps projects.

453
00:28:29,969 --> 00:28:31,265
Thanks for watching.
